WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.610
He may be the first Avenger, but he isn't
the chosen one.

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:08.210
And now we might know why, as a hilarious
new fan theory may explain just why Captain

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:13.599
America was unable to lift Thor's mystical
hammer Mjolnir in Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:17.890
As most Marvel fans know, the hammer had an
enchantment on it that only allowed it to

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:19.590
be lifted by those deemed worthy.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:23.240
"Whosoever be he worthy shall haveth the power!

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.000
Whatever, man, it's a trick!"

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.600
"Oh, please be my guest!"

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.040
In the comics, of course, Captain America
has lifted Thor's hammer several times, first

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.080
and most famously in the pages of Thor #390.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.800
In the movies, however, the only people who
have been able to lift Mjolnir are the Vision

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.320
and, of course, Thor himself - though Steve
Rogers was able to at least budge the hammer,

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.340
something even War Machine and Iron Man were
unable to do despite their incredible strength.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:50.200
"I think it moved."

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:51.840
(laughs)

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:54.060
"It moved?"

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.160
"It may have moved, I don't know."

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:59.860
So why is the movie version of Steve Rogers
unable to lift the hammer?

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:05.439
Reddit user ParameciaAntic has an insane - and
pretty funny - theory that suggests that Steve's

00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:10.159
decision to ride a motorcycle without a helmet
at the end of Avengers is proof of his total

00:01:10.159 --> 00:01:14.460
disdain for laws and authority - which other
Redditors quickly seized upon as the reason

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.080
he's unworthy to heft Mjolnir.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:17.760
"You got a helmet?"

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.620
"Oh, oh I've got a helmet."

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.140
Of course, we have our own theory.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:31.070
Sure, Captain America is worthy of lifting
the hammer, but you know who isn't?

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:31.560
The Human Torch.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.500
And those aren't the only comic book characters
Chris Evans has played - he's also appeared

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:39.750
in adaptations of Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles,
Snowpiercer, Scott Pilgrim vs. the World,

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:40.750
and The Losers.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:42.900
"It was a secret government experiment.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.060
They did stuff to me.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:47.800
Spooky stuff."

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:52.499
That's why the hammer only budged when he
tried to lift it - because only part of him

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.000
was worthy.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.140
"You know what I hate about myself…

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.280
I know what people taste like."

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:03.720
"That's gross…"

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.659
Then again, everyone has their own theory
- even the people who make the movies.

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:12.760
Back in 2015 during San Diego Comic-Con, a
fan asked Age of Ultron writer-director Joss

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:17.220
Whedon why Steve Rogers isn't considered worthy,
and why he couldn't properly lift Mjonlir.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:22.159
Whedon suggested that maybe he is worthy,
but just didn't want to lift it, saying

00:02:22.159 --> 00:02:23.159
"Are we sure?

00:02:23.159 --> 00:02:24.159
Did he fail?

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:24.860
Or did he stop?"

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:29.720
In 2016, sibling filmmakers Joe and Anthony
Russo mentioned to Huffington Post that Captain

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:33.769
America wielding Mjolnir "[...] might be a
question for future storytellers" and that

00:02:33.769 --> 00:02:36.049
it "[...] might be a little too specific for
us to answer."

00:02:36.049 --> 00:02:40.209
Back then, the Russos were chatting about
their film Captain America: Civil War, but

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:44.129
in the years since, the pair went on to direct
Avengers: Infinity War, and they're also behind

00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:45.460
the lens for Avengers 4.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:49.820
So those "future storytellers" they referenced
back then may actually be the Russo brothers

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:50.850
themselves now.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:51.850
Confusing, right?

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:56.189
But that little time paradox may actually
be in keeping with rumored plans to have Cap

00:02:56.189 --> 00:02:58.689
actually lift Mjolnir in Avengers 4.

00:02:58.689 --> 00:03:02.590
Sure, it's true that Thor's original hammer
was destroyed in Thor: Ragnarok by his evil

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:07.349
sister Hela, requiring him to make an all-new
weapon, the god-killer Stormbreaker, in Avengers:

00:03:07.349 --> 00:03:07.920
Infinity War.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.939
But according to a since-deleted Reddit post
purporting to have secret information about

00:03:11.939 --> 00:03:16.269
the plot of Avengers 4, the team will travel
back in time to fight Thanos - and after a

00:03:16.269 --> 00:03:20.959
Mjolnir-wielding Thor is struck down in battle
by Thanos, Captain America will lift the hammer

00:03:20.959 --> 00:03:24.990
and gain the power of the Thunder God for
a final showdown of epic proportions.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:29.129
No one can say for certain whether that will
definitely happen, but we wouldn't be surprised

00:03:29.129 --> 00:03:34.170
if Avengers 4 really will include some element
of time travel, bring Mjolnir back into Thor's

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:37.780
life, and give Captain America a shiny hammer
to go with his signature shield.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:42.260
After that jaw-dropping twist at the end of
Infinity War, we're expecting the unexpected

00:03:42.269 --> 00:03:44.250
when it comes to the next Avengers movie.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:45.949
We just hope it's all worth the wait.

